<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isle of Mull</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isle of Mull is the second-largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-hebrides">Inner Hebrides</a>, off the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, separated from the mainland by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">the Sound</a> of Mull. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it lay within the Gaelic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, close to the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> off its western tip, and it later belonged to the Lordship of the Isles and the Macleans of Duart. Its chief town is Tobermory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isle-of-mull.jpg" alt="Isle of Mull" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clydiee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tobermory_waterfront_recrop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mull covers 875.35 square kilometres, making it the fourth-largest island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and has a coastline of 480 km moderated by the Gulf Stream. Three large peninsulas jut west from a mountainous south-eastern core, whose highest peak, Ben More at 966 m, is the only Munro in the Scottish islands outside Skye. The Aros peninsula in the north holds the main town of Tobermory, a planned fishing town that was a burgh until 1973. Ardmeanach lies in the middle, and the Ross of Mull to the south-west, where the ferry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> leaves from Fionnphort. Off the west coast lie many smaller islands, among them Iona, Staffa, the island of Fingal's Cave, and Ulva.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mull has probably been inhabited since shortly after the last Ice Age, about 11,000 years ago, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a> of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hut at Crieth Dubh shows. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> people built brochs at Dun Nan Gall and An Sean Chaisteal, a stone circle at Lochbuie and numerous burial cairns. In the 6th century CE Irish migrants established the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, in which the Cenél Loairn controlled Mull and the adjacent mainland.</p><p>In the 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invasions destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and replaced it with the Norse Kingdom of the Isles, which became part of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> after its unification, around 872. After Robert I defeated his enemies, among them the MacDougalls, he gave Mull and other lands to Angus Og Macdonald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles">Lord of the Isles</a>, who had fought beside him at Bannockburn. The descendants of the Cenél Loairn kept their identity as the MacLeans. When John MacDonald was exiled, James IV restored their authority over Mull. Duart Castle, received as a dowry by an earlier chief, became their stronghold, and a cadet branch built a tower house at Moy.</p><p>After the Jacobite risings the Heritable Jurisdictions Act abolished comital authority in Mull, and the Campbells could only assert influence as landlords. Many MacLean castles had been slighted by the Campbells or fallen into disrepair. Gaelic, the traditional language since the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, declined in the 20th century, with a fall of 20 percent in Mull after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island's population was 3,063 in 2022, and much of it lives in Tobermory. Tourism is the mainstay of the economy, which began to revive when Craignure Pier was built in 1964. The reintroduction of white-tailed eagles in 2005 became an ecotourist attraction. Four castles stand on the island, among them Duart and the keep of Moy Castle, and there are two distilleries, at Tobermory and Tiroran. The most-used ferry runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oban">Oban</a> to Craignure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isle of Mull” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 7th century it controlled Lorn, <b>Mull</b> and neighbouring districts in northern Argyll, with its main stronghold at Dunollie near Oban.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li></ul></section>
